\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia higher, shrug off  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  jitters  \b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2006\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Dec 1 (Reuters) - Sustained capital inflows sent Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher on Friday as the U.S. dollar weakened globally, with the Thai baht <THB=> leading the rally amid signs of easing market concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Underscoring solid fundamentals buoying most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday the country's exports in November grew a faster-than-expected 19.8 percent to a monthly record from a year earlier, maintaining surprising strength.  \par \par \uc2 "The risk appetite is still very good and there is no sign that any growth slowdown is impacting inflows into the region at the moment, and the dollar is softer," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "So strong inflows plus a soft dollar, even with central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , means the trend remains in place," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Renewed strength in most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks has triggered a wave of verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by regional central banks to cap further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 "Looking ahead, renewed interests for short dollar positions should place dollar/Asia on track to end this week with another decline," \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists Claudio Piron and Yen Ping Ho at JPMorgan wrote in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas, said she remained bullish about the near-term strength of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the latest gains helped by dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a 14-year low against sterling <GBP=> on Friday and a new 20-month trough versus the euro <EUR=> as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered from expectations a weakening economy will prompt the Federal Reserve to cut interest rates next year.  \par \par \uc2 BEST PERFORMERS  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht and Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> were Friday's best performers, with the baht gaining more than two thirds of a percent to 35.71 per dollar, its strongest level in eight years.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Thailand has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  both verbally and through actual dollar buying this week, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Callum Henderson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Standard Chartered Bank, said the baht, which has gained about 15 percent so far this year, was also supported as political uncertainty after a military coup in September eased.  \par \par \uc2 "People took another look at Thailand and said okay, inflation has peaked, growth is set to accelerate so quite a few investors who were underweight Thailand have come back in the past few months," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The latest Reuters poll on positioning in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests investors stepped up long positions in the Thai baht and remained bullish on the South Korean won and Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit strengthened to about 3.5915 per dollar, up about 0.75 percent from levels traded late on Thursday. A trader said the ringgit's unusually sharp gains were largely due to technical selling of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=>, underpinned by third-quarter gross domestic product data that was revised upward, rose to as high as 927.1 per dollar, gaining moderately but still off a nine-year high this week of 925.  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> firmed as high as 32.25 per U.S. dollar, hitting its highest level in about 4-\u189\'00\'BD months, while the Singapore dollar <SGD=> briefly hit 1.5372 against the U.S. dollar, matching a nine-year high set on Thursday. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR; Change on the day at 0728 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.95         116.13       +0.16\par  Sing dlr        1.5429         1.5431       +0.01\par  Taiwan dlr      32.354         32.432       +0.24\par  Korean won      928.50         929.10       +0.06\par  Baht             35.74          36.00       +0.73\par  Peso             49.52          49.66       +0.28\par  Rupiah         9150.00        9167.00       +0.19\par  Rupee            44.70          44.74       +0.08\par  Ringgit         3.5930         3.6180       +0.70\par  Yuan            7.8308         7.8334       +0.03\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.95         117.96       +1.73\par  Sing dlr        1.5429         1.6625       +7.75\par  Taiwan dlr      32.354         32.850       +1.53\par  Korean won      928.50        1011.60       +8.95\par  Baht             35.74          41.11      +15.03\par  Peso             49.52          53.09       +7.21\par  Rupiah         9150.00        9835.00       +7.49\par  Rupee            44.70          45.04       +0.76\par  Ringgit         3.5930         3.7790       +5.18\par  Yuan            7.8308         8.0702       +3.06\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  