\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-S.Korea's Oct factory output lifts rate outlook  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Jin-joo and Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2006\par 09:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with output data, analysts, markets)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Nov 29 (Reuters) - South Korean factory output grew unexpectedly for a third consecutive month in October, data showed on Wednesday, strengthening the chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lift interest rates next year.  \par \par \uc2 The industrial output index in October rose a seasonally adjusted 2.6 percent from the previous month, compared with the median 0.8 percent drop forecast in a Reuters poll and following a revised 3.1 percent gain in September.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices gave up their early gains to turn weaker as the data, together with a separate government indicator suggesting an upbeat economic outlook, improved the prospect of a rise in interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Investors have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lift interest rates early next year, if local housing prices kept growing despite a series of government steps and as long as Asia's third-largest economy stayed on a sustained growth track.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's focus has recently been shifting toward an increasing probability of a rate increase and today's production data reinforced the probability even further," said Kong Dong-rak, a fixed-income analyst at SK Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Industrial output in October rose 4.6 percent from a year earlier, data from the National Statistical Office showed, below a 5.8 percent gain forecast in the Reuters survey.  \par \par \uc2 But the effect of slower annual growth was outweighed by strong monthly growth and the statistics agency's separate data showing the economy's outlook had improved in October.  \par \par \uc2 The agency said its leading composite index -- compiled from 10 types of indicators about the future -- rose 4.5 percent in October from a year earlier, accelerating from a 4.1 percent rise in September. It releases only the growth rates.  \par \par \uc2 SOFTER U.S. ECONOMY, FIRMER WON  \par \par \uc2 Treasury bond December futures <KTBc1> gave up more than an earlier gain of 13 ticks to close down four ticks at 108.67.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said South Korean exports were expected to fare better next year than many had earlier predicted as the effect of a slowing U.S. economy would be offset by robust economies elsewhere, such as Europe and China.  \par \par \uc2 "The figures showed South Korea's economy is in very good shape, led by strong exports, and that U.S. and global economies are healthy," said Oh Suk-tae, an economist at Citigroup. "The Bank of Korea may consider raising interest rates, given the bubble in South Korea's property market."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts do not expect the Bank of Korea to raise interest rates at its Dec. 7 policy meeting after its surprise upgrade in required cash reserve ratios announced on Nov. 23.  \par \par \uc2 The commerce ministry was also optimistic about exports, as it said on Wednesday that this year's shipments abroad would probably total about $326 billion, up 14.6 percent from 2005 and above a target set at $318 billion. [ID:nSEO150476]  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the firmer won would not have a big impact on South Korea's exports, unlike in recent years, although the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hurt profitability at export industries.  \par \par \uc2 But the firmer won has hit the current account surplus, which plunged to a seasonally adjusted $1.97 billion for the first 10 months of this year from $14.12 billion a year earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier on Wednesday. [ID:nSEO184210]  \par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> has gained more than 8 percent this year to hit its strongest levels in more than nine years against both the dollar and the yen this month. It has since fallen back slightly.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers had said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month to contain the won's strength, including about an estimated $1 billion purchase on Wednesday alone.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Lee Soo-jung and Cheon Jong-woo)  \par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061129e2bt000py\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
