\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Korea Keeps Pressure On KRW; Bonds Rally  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jin-Young Yook  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2006\par 10:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean authorities Wednesday continued to put pressure on the won through verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and warned bond issuance could be increased to support dollar-buying efforts.  \par \par \uc2 The moves, following through on Tuesday's dollar-buying and tough talk, pushed the dollar a little higher against the won, but sent government bonds rallying as market expectations grow for the Bank of Korea to keep the call rate unchanged next month.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark five-year treasury bond yield, which started the day's session higher at 5.14%, fell to finish at 5.06%. The dollar, meanwhile, ended at KRW946.5, up from Tuesday's close of KRW945.0.  \par \par \uc2 According to traders, the government was suspected to have bought about $500 million worth of dollars against the won early in the morning, about the same amount at Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Later, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Minister of Planning and Budget Byeon Yang-Kyoon said the government may consider increasing its ceiling on treasury bond issues for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stabilization.  \par \par \uc2 "The sharp fall in the (dollar-won) \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate and rise in international oil prices have reached a serious level, and should be dealt with," Byeon said.  \par \par \uc2 "Financial authorities don't like to see an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stabilization bond issues because it burdens the fiscal budget, but the government can raise the ceiling on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bond issues next year" from its current likely level of KRW10 trillion, said Byeon.  \par \par \uc2 The government's ceiling on treasury issuance for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purposes has been set at KRW11 trillion for this year.  \par \par \uc2 Although the news is potentially bearish for bonds, the market didn't react much as traders considered any increase would not be immediate.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, reported comments by Bank of Korea Governor Lee Seongtae at a closed-door meeting bolstered the bond market. According to Yonhap Infomax, Lee said, "It is doubtful that (exporting) companies can endure (the dollar-won exchange rate) at such a level."  \par \par \uc2 "The market is thinking that if companies are having such a hard time, it will be difficult for the Bank of Korea to consider tightening the call rate soon," said a trader at a local securities firm.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, "market players are betting highly on the BOK holding the policy interest rate steady (in May)," said a trader at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy committee meeting is scheduled to take place May 11. The overnight call rate target is now at 4.00%.  \par \par \uc2 Although the authorities seem intent on pushing the won down further, Korea's robust economy continues to support a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For instance, Citigroup economist Oh Suktae expects the dollar to move back to KRW940 in the next few weeks, and for five-year treasurys to yield 5.20%.  \par \par \uc2 Kwon Tae-Kyun, director general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy's international finance bureau, told Dow Jones Newswires Tuesday the won's rise should slow even though the Japanese yen is strengthening sharply.  \par \par \uc2 As the won has risen 7.6% against the U.S. unit so far this year versus a relatively steady yen during the same period, "this is not the time for the won to be tracking the yen" up, Kwon said.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, he said, "the dollar/won level should take this opportunity to recover when the dollar/yen is dropping, so that the yen/won cross rate can regain above 8.5."  \par \par \uc2 At 0600 GMT, the yen was at KRW8.2300, slightly down from Tuesday's KRW8.2483, as the dollar recovered to above Y115.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Jin-Young Yook, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; jin-young.yook@dowjones.com \par    (Eun-Young Sa and Jung-Ah Lee contributed to this story.) \par    -Edited by Lim Mui Khi \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 26-04-06 0841GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060426e24q000nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
