\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Korean won, Taiwan dollar shine  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2006\par 04:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 6 (Reuters) - The South Korean won rose to its highest level in 8-\u189\'00\'BD years on Thursday and the Taiwan dollar pushed higher as well on the back of strong foreign demand for local equities.  \par \par \uc2 Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed and largely ignored a softer tone in the Japanese yen <JPY=>. Markets in Thailand and India were closed for a holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=><KRW=KFTC> rose as far as 951.6 per dollar, up about 0.6 percent from Wednesday's close in local trade and the strongest level since October 1997.  \par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister Bahk Byong-won said on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there were abnormal signs such as speculative trading.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers said firm demand for local shares, optimism about the economy and a feeling that U.S. interest rates may not rise much further meant that further gains in the won were likely.  \par \par \uc2 "Won strength has picked up since the end of March. This has coincided with broad dollar weakness, but it is also because of inflows and expectations for more rate hikes in Korea when the U.S. is unlikely to lift rates too much further," said Jan Lambregts, head of Asia-Pacific research at Rabobank.  \par \par \uc2 "We are looking for dollar/won to come off substantially."  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> added half a percent to 32.30 per U.S. dollar from Tuesday's domestic close. Taiwan's financial markets were closed on Wednesday for a holiday.  \par \par \uc2 A rally in Taiwan's key stock index <.TWII> lifted expectations of foreign demand for local shares.  \par \par \uc2 In Seoul, the stock market <.KS11> closed up 0.59 percent, gaining for the eleventh consecutive session, with foreign investors buying a net 125.5 billion won ($131.1 million) of shares on the main exchange by midday.  \par \par \uc2 THE YUAN FACTOR  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said expectations of a pick-up in the pace of appreciation in the Chinese yuan <CNY=CFX> also bolstered regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yuan firmed to its highest level in a month in offshore forwards trading as this week's sharp rise in the spot exchange rate encouraged investors to bet on speedier \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.  \par \par \uc2 The one-year non-deliverable forward (NDF) <CNYNDFOR=> moved to about 7.6900 yuan per dollar, the strongest since early March, compared with 7.7150 late on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar weakness and expectations that Beijing would tolerate further yuan strength ahead of President Hu Jintao's visit to the United States this month have boosted the yuan this week.  \par \par \uc2 "The moves we've seen in the NDF market are a reaction to the spot move," said a Singapore-based NDF trader. "Much of it is to do with the euro's strength against the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> was steady after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged on Wednesday but said they could be cut earlier than expected as inflation eased.  \par \par \uc2 Annual inflation in March stood at 15.74 percent, the lowest level since fuel prices surged last October.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah was at 9,000 per dollar, little moved from levels seen in late Asian trade on Wednesday and off a 16-month peak at about 8,970 hit earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Economists had been expecting Bank Indonesia's key interest rate, which stands at 12.75 percent after six rises since its introduction last July, to be cut in the second half of this year.  \par \par \uc2 Craig Chan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at the Royal Bank of Scotland in Hong Kong, said rate cuts this year could provide a further boost to the rupiah since it would reflect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confidence that inflation was on its way down.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as potential rate cuts are gradual, they should be positive for the rupiah," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Rate cuts could support equity market inflows, and on the bond side they could stimulate more demand as people try to lock in high rates before any change in policy." \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day 0755 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.55         116.97       -0.49\par  Sing dlr        1.6050         1.6068       +0.11\par  Taiwan dlr      32.323         32.472       +0.46\par  Korean won      953.40         957.30       +0.41\par  Peso             51.12          51.09       -0.06\par  Rupiah         9000.00        8999.00       -0.01\par  Ringgit         3.6670         3.6710       +0.11\par  Yuan            8.0082         8.0083       +0.00\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.55         117.96       +0.35\par  Sing dlr        1.6050         1.6625       +3.58\par  Taiwan dlr      32.323         32.850       +1.63\par  Korean won      953.40        1011.60       +6.10\par  Peso             51.12          53.09       +3.85\par  Rupiah         9000.00        9835.00       +9.28\par  Ringgit         3.6670         3.7790       +3.05\par  Yuan            8.0082         8.0702       +0.77 ((Reporting\par   