\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea c.bank faces 2006 net loss on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, March 6 (Reuters) - South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to suffer a third consecutive yearly loss in 2006 on costs related to heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an official at the Bank of Korea said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported a 1.87 trillion won ($1.93 billion) net loss in the year ended Dec. 31, 2005 after losing 150.22 billion won in 2004, when it recorded its first loss in 10 years, said the official.  \par \par \uc2 "Increased costs related to monetary stabilisation bonds and the dollar/won exchange rate's drop were the main causes for the loss last year," said the official, who declined to be named.  \par \par \uc2 Monetary stabilisation bonds (MSBs), issued to absorb liquidity partly due to dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , jumped 9 percent to an outstanding 155.2 trillion won at the end of 2005 from a year earlier, he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market heavily between late 2003 and early 2005 to keep the won <KRW=> down against the dollar due to fears that a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hurt exports, the main driver of growth in Asia's fourth-largest economy in recent years.  \par \par \uc2 It would not be possible to slash quickly the outstanding amount of MSBs this year and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably suffer another net loss, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which manages the world's fourth-largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves valued at $215.95 billion at the end of February, also lost out on the value of its reserves in won terms because of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar last year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained 11.8 percent on average against the dollar in 2005 from the previous year.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea has more than sufficient internal reserves to cover the losses, but the deficit sparked calls from politicians and the media for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from the middle of 2005. ($1=970.9 Won)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-KOREA-CBANK|LANGEN|ABN|M|D  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060309e2360005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
