\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Sth Korea optimistic on growth, plays down won risk  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2006\par 03:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts after news conference, adds details)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Feb 23 (Reuters) - Accelerating domestic demand means South Korea's 2006 economic growth target of 5 percent is achievable despite the risks from a firmer won and higher oil prices, Finance Minister Han Duck-soo said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Han also said annual growth in exports would return to a double-digit rate in February after plunging far below 10 percent to the slowest pace in nearly three years in January.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking at his ministry's weekly media conference, he said the impact of the won's <KRW=> value on exports was less than it once had been, underlining the government's recent attitude of showing more tolerance on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term movements.  \par \par \uc2 Han said growth in domestic demand was accelerating, with recent indicators of private consumption, the most important element in domestic demand, pointing to continued expansion.  \par \par \uc2 "Taking into account the recent trend, we don't expect any big difficulty in achieving growth of around 5 percent," said Han. South Korea's economy grew 4 percent in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its target interest rate three times in five months, most recently on Feb. 9, to head off the risk of inflation rising as the economy gathered strength.  \par \par \uc2 January exports rose 3.8 percent from a year earlier, the slowest pace in nearly three years.  \par \par \uc2 Economists were wary of reading too much into the data because it was distorted by the Lunar New Year holidays, which had fallen on February in 2005, and Han said exports were robust.  \par \par \uc2 "We still view exports as maintaining a sound momentum because exports are expected to recover at a double-digit growth rate in February with the daily average exports staying above $1 billion," Han said.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea is home to global suppliers of chips, cars, ships and mobile phones such as Samsung Electronics Co. Ltd. , Hyundai Motor Co. , Hyundai Heavy Industries Co. and LG Electronics Inc. .  \par \par \uc2 The won has gained about 4 percent against the dollar so far this year on top of a 12 percent gain on average in 2005, prompting officials from the commerce ministry to criticise the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as being too complacent.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed away from massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since early last year, after criticism from politicians and media about the costs paid for capping the won's strength in the previous few years.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-KOREA (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060223e22n0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
