\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  rally may resume on growth optimism  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Rajat Bhattacharya  \par \uc2 980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2006\par 04:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats item that first ran on Thursday)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 2 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have paused after a breathless run-up in January, but analysts said on Thursday the rally would soon resume given the strength of investment inflows into the region's markets.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Thailand, the Philippines, Indonesia and South Korea, among the world's best performers against the dollar so far this year, still rank among the top picks in Asia, they said.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Singapore dollar and the Taiwan dollar might struggle for gains in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 The strategists' call came even as the dollar struck a seven-week high against the yen <JPY=> as markets bet U.S. short-term rates would peak at 5 percent, instead of 4.25-4.5 percent predicted late last year.  \par \par \uc2 "On balance the story remains positive for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," wrote Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Equity flow data continue to show net inflows into the region, while the drop in oil prices overnight lowers the threat posed by perhaps the most obvious risk factor likely to derail Asia -- energy prices."  \par \par \uc2 Equity inflows have enabled Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter the dollar's strength against the yen and euro <EUR=>, pushing some of them to their highest levels in years as they overcome sporadic dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local central banks.  \par \par \uc2 More than $3 billion in foreign investment has flown into Thai stocks <.SETI>, $2.3 billion into South Korean stocks, $1.2 billion into Taiwan <.TWII> and $820 million into India <.BSESN> since the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time dealers said authorities in South Korea, Thailand, Singapore and Taiwan have bought billions of dollars to stall gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exports.  \par \par \uc2 In a sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jumped $6.54 billion in January, marking their biggest monthly gain in 14 months.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE ROADBLOCK  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=>, in particular, faces a road-block from local authorities as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have hit the top end of a band used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) to guide the trade-weighted exchange rate, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "As such, we would not push for much more aggressive gains over the short-term," Swiss bank UBS said in a report.  \par \par \uc2 "However, we still think that the Singapore dollar will perform well on a medium-term view given the potential for the MAS to adopt a more aggressive tightening bias should the strong data continue."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the region could see more investment inflows since economists are upgading their growth forecasts for most Asian countries on the back of stronger exports and growing domestic consumption.  \par \par \uc2 The improved outlook has already stoked investors' appetite for taking on higher risks. Many have borrowed low-interest bearing yen to buy Asian stocks, bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "This reflects the fact that low-yielding yen is currently a global funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a risk-loving environment, while Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are beneficiaries of risk-seeking global capital seeking returns in their asset markets," Jalinoos wrote.  \par \par \uc2 The quest for higher returns is reflected in the increased purchase of foreign bonds by Japanese investors, tired of getting low returns on local debt.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, Korean investors have focused domestically, driving Seoul's benchmark stock index <.KS11> to record highs and leaving little for them to invest overseas.  \par \par \uc2 This contrast is one of the reasons the won rose to an eight-year high of about 8.14 per yen <JPYKRW=R> this week.  \par \par \uc2 The yen might strengthen in the medium term, if the Bank of Japan tightens monetary policy later in the year, Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Banking, said in a report. "But short-term, Korean won should find buyers on dips on crosses including (vs) the yen, particularly if the Bank of Korea hikes (rates) again at the Feb. 9 meeting, as seems likely," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Rising rates, against a backdrop of persistently strong growth and inflationary pressures, would support high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>, Philippine peso <PHP=> and Thai baht <THB=>, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 But the same holds true for the U.S. dollar as the Fed shows no signs of ending its 19-month rate-rising cycle. The Fed's rate increases were the primary reason the dollar outperformed most world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2005 <=USD>, confounding many analysts.  \par \par \uc2 "The key issue for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  is whether Asia, or the U.S. dollar, is rewarded for this growth performance," wrote Richard Yetsenga, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 "Asia may be a leveraged play on global growth, but in many ways so is the U.S., with the most active major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and high average growth rates."  \par \par \uc2 Yetsenga expects a clearer indication once the latest U.S. non-farm payrolls data is out on Friday and incoming Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke delivers testimony before Congress on Feb. 15.  \par \par \uc2 If the jobs data is weak, Yetsenga sees the dollar weakening against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Thai baht, Korean won and the high yielders should again lead the move," he said. ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change so far in 2006 (1041 GMT)   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.42         117.96       -0.39\par  Sing dlr        1.6334         1.6625       +1.78\par  Taiwan dlr      31.975         32.850       +2.74\par  Korean won      972.30        1011.60       +4.04\par  Baht             39.37          41.11       +4.43\par  Peso             52.08          53.09       +1.94\par  Rupiah         9345.00        9835.00       +5.24\par  Rupee            44.28          45.04       +1.73\par  Ringgit         3.7480         3.7790       +0.83\par  Yuan            8.0616         8.0702       +0.11\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -OUTLOOK (ANALYSIS, REPEAT)|LANGEN|SI|IF|PHD|SNS|SNI|Z|J  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060203e2230005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
