\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb 2  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2006\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 MATSUYAMA, Japan: Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Toshiro Muto said Thursday that an end to nearly five years of ultra-easy monetary policy is approaching, though it is still too early to say that all the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's conditions for a policy shift have been met. ("=UPDATE: BOJ Muto Sees Chances Of Easy Policy End Growing," published at 0826 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, fell to 52.1 in January from 54.3 in December, the China Federation of Logistics & Purchasing said. ("China Jan Purchasing Managers Index 52.1 Vs Dec 54.3," published at 0419 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Group of Eight finance ministers are likely to discuss next week how recent political events in the Middle East could affect the global economy, a former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Thursday. ("Japan Ex-MOF Mizoguchi: Politics May Be Big Topic At G8," published at 0835 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Hopes that Australia's housing sector was starting to recover from a sustained soft patch were snuffed out Thursday as the number of residential building approvals slumped sharply in December. ("=DATA VIEW: Hopes Of Aussie Housing Rebound Snuffed Out," published at 0131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        CANBERRA: Australia's Deputy Prime Minister Mark Vaile Thursday defended an inquiry into Australian wheat exporter AWB Ltd.'s role in paying massive kickbacks to Saddam Hussein's regime against concerns raised by several U.S. legislators. ("=2nd UPDATE: Australian Min Defends Iraqi Wheat Inquiry," published at 0745 GMT) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL: South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves soared by its widest monthly margin in over a year to another record at the end of January, on what the Bank of Korea Thursday said were heavier yen and euro holdings. ("=UPDATE: Korea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Reserves At $216.9B; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Cited," published at 0452 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's December service sector output and January department store sales showed sharp increases, indicating a recovery in domestic consumption is accelerating. ("=UPDATE: Svc, Store Sales Show Korea Consumption Recovery," published at 0727 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL: Major South Korean companies expect further improvements in business conditions in February, though that optimism has declined slightly, according to a survey released Thursday by an interest group for conglomerates. ("South Korea Feb Business Survey Remains Optimistic - FKI," published at 0244 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: The Business Council of Australia took aim at the Treasury Department this week, claiming repeatedly inaccurate budget forecasts have thwarted tax reform. Not surprisingly, Treasury Secretary Ken Henry wasted no time defending his department, saying the comments did not bear scrutiny. Treasurer Peter Costello also took a swing back at the peak business lobby group. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Treasury Not The Culprit In Tax Go-Slow," published at 0520 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG: The value of retail sales in Hong Kong in December rose by 6.9% from a year earlier to $19.3 billion after accounting for price changes. (DATA VIEW to run by 1000 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANGKOK: Germany's KfW hopes to be able to sell its maiden bonds denominated in Thai baht in the first half of the year, with a debut issue in Chinese yuan not likely until 2007, according to a senior executive at the Frankfurt-based body. ("=INTERVIEW: Germany's KfW Hopes For 1H THB Bond Sale," published at 0549 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Indonesian U.S. dollar bonds are set for a correction following their strong performance since the sub-investment-grade country's August \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis, according to Holger Friedrich, a fund manager with Union Investment, Germany's second-largest asset manager. ("=INTERVIEW: Indonesia USD Bonds Set To Weaken - Fund Mgr," published at 0714 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Premiums collected by Singapore's life insurance industry fell 6% last year despite the privatization of two government insurance schemes, the city-state's Life Insurance Association said Thursday. ("Singapore Insurance Premiums Dn 6% In 2005-Industry Group," published at 0355 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANGKOK: Thailand's bond market is likely to double in size to THB6.6 trillion by 2010, due in part to an increase in issues to finance the government's infrastructure megaproject and businesses' capacity expansion, the country's bond market watchdog said Thursday. ("Thai Bond Mkt Size To Double To THB6.6T In 5 Yrs -Agency," published at 0804 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EQUITIES  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MELBOURNE: Global miner Rio Tinto PLC Thursday said annual net profit jumped 58% and that it plans to return US$4 billion of its bulging cash reserves to shareholders. ("=UPDATE: Rio Tinto FY Profit Surges, Plans US$4B Return," published at 0732 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Nissan Motor Co. said its net profit edged higher by 0.6% in its fiscal third quarter, with earnings supported by a weak yen against the dollar and steady sales despite a relative lack of new car releases during the period. ("=UPDATE: Nissan 3Q Net Edges Up On Weak Yen, Steady Sales," published at 0813 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial Co. said Thursday its October-December profits soared on strong sales of audiovisual goods such as plasma display panel televisions and digital cameras. ("=UPDATE:Matsushita Elec Posts Strong 3Q, Lifts FY Outlook," published at 0841 GMT; 2nd UPDATE to run after 0900 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Airlines to report fiscal third-quarter results. (Story to run after 0900 GMT followed by UPDATE)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON: Telecom Corp. of New Zealand Ltd. Thursday reported a big fiscal second quarter loss as it halved the book value of its struggling Australian businesses, but the company's performance in its home market won favor with investors. ("=UPDATE: NZ Telecom Reports 2Q Loss, But NZ Ops Surprise," published at 0035 GMT; see also "=INTERVIEW: NZ Telecom Wants Quick Australian Ops Review," published at 0410 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Eisai Co. Thursday raised its group net profit forecast for the fiscal year to March by 5% as global sales of prescription drugs for Alzheimer's disease and ulcers exceeded its expectations. ("=UPDATE: Eisai Lifts FY Earnings, Dividend Outlook," published at 0700 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Motors Corp. said Thursday that it will announce a tie-up with Malaysia's Proton Holdings Bhd. on Friday. ("Mitsubishi Motors To Announce Tie-Up With Proton Friday," published at 0851 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG: Asia Financial Holdings Ltd., a small Hong Kong banking insurance group, said Thursday it shortlisted nine bidders for its wholly owned banking unit. ("HK Asia Financial Shortlists 9 Bidders For Banking Unit," published at 0036 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG: Chinese household appliances retailer GOME Electrical Appliances Holding Ltd. said Thursday it agreed to sell a 9.71% stake to U.S. private equity group Warburg Pincus LLC for US$150 million. ("Warburg Pincus To Buy 9.71% Of China GOME For US$150 Mln," published at 0835 GMT; UPDATE to run around 1200 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG: Ports-to-telecommunications conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa Ltd. will have to further delay listing its Italian third-generation mobile operator if a Milan regulator fails to approve the deal in two weeks, a person familiar with the situation said Thursday. ("3 Italia Seen Delaying IPO If No OK Before Feb 15-Sources," published at 0710 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL: U.S. investment bank Merrill Lynch & Co. is interested in buying controlling stake in South Korea's leading credit card issuer LG Card Co. , said a source familiar with the matter on Thursday. ("Merrill Interested In Bidding For S Korea LG Card- Source," published at 0623 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SYDNEY: Australia's Adelaide Bank Ltd. said Thursday its net profit for the six months ending Dec. 31 rose 22% to A$41.4 million from A$33.9 million in the year earlier period. ("Australia's Adelaide Bk 1H Net A$41.4M, Dividend 26 Cents," published at 0049 GMT; see also "=INTERVIEW: Adelaide Bank Keeps Market Share Target," published at 0504 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Denso Corp. said Thursday that its group profits for the October-December period climbed on year thanks to brisk sales and cost-cutting efforts. ("Japan's Denso Oct-Dec Group Net Profit Y56.7B Vs Y39.8B," published at 0256 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Asahi Kasei Corp. said Thursday that its group profits for the nine-month period ended Dec. 31 fell on year due to pension related expenses. ("Asahi Kasei 9 Mos Grp Net Y46.58B Vs Y46.70B," published at 0531 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Electric Corp. said Thursday its October-December profits grew thanks to robust demand for factory-related production equipment and improved profitability in its electronic device business. ("M'bishi Electric Oct-Dec Group Net Pft Y32.3B Vs Y17.2B," published at 0500 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Kubota Corp. , a Japanese maker of agricultural machinery, said Thursday its group net profit fell 4.8% in April-December from a year earlier, when its tax burden was lighter due to restructuring-related moves. ("Japan's Kubota Apr-Dec Group Net Profit Y65.3B Vs Y68.6B," published at 0642 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Airport Terminal Services to report third-quarter results. (Story to run after 0900 GMT).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: SIA Engineering to report third-quarter results. (Story to run after 0900 GMT).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANGKOK: Interview with Singapore-listed Total Access Communication PCL's chief executive. (Story to run by 1000 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Mazda Motor Corp. President Hisakazu Imaki said Thursday his company will introduce its first crossover sport-utility vehicle in Japan by the end of the year. ("Mazda Pres: To Introduce New SUV In Japan By Year-end," published at 0557 GMT) MELBOURNE: West Australian Newspapers Holdings Ltd. Thursday reported a 50% fall in first half net profit, largely due to a provision for redundancy payments and the accounting impact of an investment in new printing equipment. ("West Australian Newspapers 1H Net A$24.0M," published at 0612 GMT) [ 02-02-06 0907GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL: The ratio of non-performing loans at South Korean banks for end-2005 fell to 1.22%, its lowest level since the government began keeping records six years ago, the Financial Supervisory Service said Thursday. ("South Korea Banks' Bad Loan Ratio Falls To 1.22%, New Low," published at 2100 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MANILA: Philippine Savings Bank , or PSBank, on Thursday posted higher 2005 earnings, on the back of double-digit loan growth. ("Philippine Savings Bank 2005 Net Pft PHP640M Vs PHP485M," published at 0500 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        SINGAPORE: Copper, zinc and aluminum all hit fresh peaks in Asia Thursday as funds continued to pour money into commodities, supported by expectations of tight supply on the back of shortfalls in production. ("=UPDATE: Copper, Zinc, Aluminum Hit Fresh Peaks In Asia," published at 0519 GMT) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CANBERRA: Australian units of Rio Tinto and Xstrata will supply the Mexican Federal Electricity Commission with a total 8.84 million metric tons of thermal coal over 18 months in a deal worth A$688 million, Resources Minister Ian Macfarlane announced Thursday. ("Australian Govt: Rio Tinto, Xstrata In Mexico Coal Deal," published at 2245 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING: China is setting up a National Petroleum Reserve Center to better manage four planned strategic petroleum reserve bases, according to a recent report by the National Development and Reform Commission. ("China Setting Up Oil Reserve Management Center -Reports," published at 0433 GMT) [ 02-02-06 0907GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060202e222000ez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
