\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won hits 8-year high, rupiah and baht surge  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Rajat Bhattacharya  \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2006\par 04:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The South Korean won hit an eight-year-high on Thursday while the Indonesian rupiah and Thai baht rose to their strongest levels since last March as the improving economic outlook in Asia boosted demand for stocks.  \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> strengthened beyond 1.62 per dollar to a 10-1/2-month high in overnight trading, nearly hitting its highest level since 1998, but caution over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  led it to pull back during Asian trading hours.  \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht <THB=> jumped as much as 1.1 percent to 38.8 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> edged towards a 2-1/2-year high of 52.25 per dollar hit on Jan. 9.  \par \par \uc2  The gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  contrasted with a decline in the yen <JPY=> against the dollar and indicated investors' appetite for taking risks in emerging markets, where growth is likely to outperform the developed countries.  \par \par \uc2  Investors have flocked to Asian stock markets this year on optimism that the region would grow in 2006 at a faster pace than previously anticipated thanks to an upturn in exports and a revival in domestic consumption.  \par \par \uc2  "There's been strong inflows into the region this month," said James Malcolm, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  "It's not just speculative money. There's a large amount of money going into places like Korea based on strong fundamentals. Growth is good, inflation pressures are certainly there."  \par \par \uc2  Reuters polls of Asia's 10 biggest economies outside Japan released on Wednesday showed that economists had upgraded their growth forecasts for all countries except Taiwan and Malaysia since the previous poll conducted in October.  \par \par \uc2  Malcolm said China and Malaysia were likely to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate at a faster pace starting in February or March, which would also tend to push up other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  "We're particularly positive on the peso, reasonably positive on Korea and positive on Taiwan and Thailand," he said.  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> touched 1.6172 per dollar in overnight trade but then fell back to levels weaker than 1.62.  A breach of 1.6162 per dollar would take the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its strongest level since October 1998.  \par \par \uc2  Some dealers suggested that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker than the 1.62-per-dollar level.  \par \par \uc2  "They're checking around prices, so everybody's being cautious in bidding it higher," said a Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer.  \par \par \uc2  "I think the market is oversold (on dollars) so there will be a slight correction."  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore conducts monetary policy by guiding the Singapore dollar's trade-weighted exchange rate within an undisclosed band.  \par \par \uc2  Malcolm said the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be allowed to move higher if other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened.  \par \par \uc2  "They're not trying to draw a line in the sand," he said.  \par \par \uc2  But, as of now, the Singapore dollar was expensive, compared with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its trade partners, he said.  \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  which forced the won to retreat from its eight-year high of 965.9 per dollar.  The Indonesian rupiah strengthened to its highest level since March 3, 2005 at 9,285 per dollar, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rallied from a four-year low of 11,750 per dollar in August as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed up key interest rates to 12.75 percent to curb inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said that the interest rates paid on local bonds, among the highest in Asia, were attracting foreign investors. Money has also been flowing into Jakarta stocks.  \par \par \uc2  "It's related to stock and debt inflows," said one dealer in the Indonesian capital. "We suspect Singapore banks are actively selling dollar-rupiah." \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Change on the day at   0802 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.56         114.94       -0.54\par  Sing dlr        1.6219         1.6210       -0.06\par  Taiwan dlr      31.961         31.970       +0.03\par  Korean won      968.90         968.90       +0.00\par  Baht             38.83          39.22       +1.00\par  Peso             52.36          52.46       +0.19\par  Rupiah         9340.00        9400.00       +0.64\par  Ringgit         3.7445         3.7460       +0.04\par  Yuan            8.0620         8.0619       -0.00 Change so\par far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.56         117.96       +2.08\par  Sing dlr        1.6219      