\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won at highest in nearly 7 months, exporters buy  \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2006\par 02:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to midday)  \par \par \uc2 0340 GMT - South Korean won <KRW=> firmer on steady dollar sales by local exporters, hitting its highest against the U.S. dollar in nearly seven months, but caution over possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caps its gains.  \par \par \uc2 - Won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> quoted at 1,005.0 per dollar, up 0.3 percent from Monday's domestic close of 1,008.0 per dollar. It touched 1,004.5 at one point, its highest since 1,003.9 on June 10.  \par \par \uc2 - Companies sell the U.S. dollar for won to settle their export deals, while offshore investors increase won buying, dealers say.  \par \par \uc2 - Commerce ministry forecasts South Korean exports will grow 11.7 percent in 2006 due to the robust world economy and rising sales of chips and mobile phones. Exports grew 12.2 percent in 2005 and 31 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 - However, the won faces resistance around 1,005 per dollar as investors remain wary of possible dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  although no move by the authorities was spotted, dealers say.  \par \par \uc2 - "The dollar can fall as low as 1,002 soon if the government does not step in to support it," a local bank dealer says.  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose in December because a softer dollar lifted the value of assets held in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers also cited sporadic dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities in late December as a factor.  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's fourth largest, reached $210.39 billion at the end of December.  \par \par \uc2 - Yen <JPY=>, which the won usually follows closely, quoted at 117.35/39 per dollar, compared with 117.63/71 late on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 - Seoul's benchmark stock index <.KS11> down 0.58 percent, with foreigners selling 729 million won of shares on the main board.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      0340 GMT      0120 GMT    prev close\par  Won <KRW=KFTC>          1,005.0       1,006.8       1,008.0\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>    8.5625/64     8.5429/60     8.5640/23\par  NDF 1-mth won       1,004.2/5.2   1,006.0/7.0   1,007.1/8.1\par  NDF 3-mth won       1,003.4/4.4   1,005.2/6.2   1,006.3/7.3\par  KOSPI <.KS11>          1,381.15      1,383.34      1,389.27\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060106e2130002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
