\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won flat, dealers cite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2005\par 01:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to noon)  \par \par \uc2 0329 GMT - South Korean won <KRW=> virtually unchanged, as heavy dollar offers by exporters near the year-end are absorbed by sustained bids for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dealers put down to the authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 - Won <KRW=KFTC> quoted at 1,012.8 per dollar, compared with Tuesday's local finish of 1,012.5.  \par \par \uc2 - The local unit was held between 1,012.2 and 1,013.9 in morning trade, after data showing the country's current account surplus narrowed in November and a projection for a further fall in the balance this month."  \par \par \uc2 - "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar could have slipped to 1,003-1,004," a local bank dealer says.  \par \par \uc2 - "Given that the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying the other day was not minimal and sporadic buying by the authorities today, it is unlikely exporters' deals will push the dollar lower."  \par \par \uc2 - The Bank of Korea declined to comment on speculation about dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 - Dealers said they expect the dollar to find support at 1,010 by the end of this week due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account surplus dropped to $2.05 billion in November from a $2.82 billion surplus a year earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. [ID:nSLT000988]  \par \par \uc2 - The surplus was seen declining further to some $1.4 billion in December, an official at the Bank of Korea says. [ID:nSEO6712]  \par \par \uc2 - Dollar keeps sharp gains against the yen in U.S. trade as investors remain convinced Japan will continue near-zero interest rates for a long time to come.  \par \par \uc2 - South Korea expects net capital outflows and easier access to local borrowing for foreigners to take upward pressure off the won <KRW=>, a senior finance ministry official says.  \par \par \uc2 - Kwon Tae-kyun, director general of the ministry's international finance bureau, also tells Reuters in an interview that China is unlikely to revalue the yuan <CNY=CFXS> sharply and that the impact on the won <KRW=> from any revaluation will not be that big anyway.  \par \par \uc2 - Yen <JPY=>, which the won usually follows closely, quoted at 117.29/39 per dollar, little changed from 117.39 in late U.S. trade on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 - Japan's industrial output rose less-than-expected 1.4 percent in November from a month earlier, government data showed, but forecasts released with the data pointed to solid growth in the months ahead, suggesting the economy remains in an uptrend.  \par \par \uc2 - South Korea is set to release industrial production data on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 - Seoul's main stock index <.KS11> dips 0.47 percent, after hitting an all-time high on Tuesday. Foreign investors sell a net 86 billion won on the main board.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    0329 GMT       0040 GMT     prev close\par  Won <KRW=KFTC>        1,012.8        1,012.6        1,012.5\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>  8.6273/46      8.6222/04      8.6350/15\par  NDF 1-mth won     1,011.9/2.9    1,012.6/3.6    1,011.6/2.6\par  NDF 3-mth won     1,011.1/2.1    1,011.8/2.8    1,010.8/1.8\par  KOSPI <.KS11>        1,367.06       1,358.23       1,373.55\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051231e1cs0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\