\par\par \b \uc2 Yen tumbles to 20-year low on BOJ index  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs  \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2005\par 08:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 21 (Reuters) - The yen hit a 20-year low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major trading partners in early November on a Bank of Japan index, underscoring weakness that should help spur a recovery from deflation in Japan's economy.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ's real effective exchange rate index, which gauges the yen's trade-weighted and inflation-adjusted performance, fell to 106.2 by Nov. 17, its lowest since October 1985. The index was updated on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The low level reflects the yen's broad slide against a variety of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but also nearly eight years of deflation, which has made Japanese goods cheaper compared with those of countries where prices have been rising.  \par \par \uc2 Such a drop in the yen should make Japanese exports more attractive and buttress an economic rebound increasingly being helped by consumer and business spending. Japan's exports hit a record 5.925 trillion yen ($49.72 billion) in September.  \par \par \uc2 "The main significance is the impact on export competitiveness," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at Macquarie Securities Japan. "There's quite nice support for the economic recovery coming from the exchange rate."  \par \par \uc2 The yen has suffered as more than four years of near zero short-term interest rates has prompted many Japanese investors to put cash to work in higher-yielding foreign bonds this year, driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a two-year low versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the yen has fared even worse, tumbling to seven-year lows against the British pound, Australian dollar and South Korean won this week, as well as an eight-year nadir against the New Zealand dollar on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's once massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also seen as a factor behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's woes after the government flooded markets with yen to prevent a sharp appreciation that could exacerbate deflation.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF sold 35 trillion yen in the 15 months to March 2004 but has since stayed out of the market. With no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday the MOF will have gone its longest without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since records began to be kept in 1991.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's depreciation has been enough to prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  to threaten \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Vice Finance Minister Kwon Tae-shin telling Reuters on Monday that Seoul is worried about the won's rise and will act if it is deemed excessive.  \par \par \uc2 Tohru Umemoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in Tokyo, said in a recent report that the level of the yen index means Japanese manufacturers are enjoying their best comparative price advantage since the Plaza Accord of 1985.  \par \par \uc2 The Plaza Accord was an agreement between Japan, the United States, France, Britain and the then West Germany that included coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the dollar and help reduce the U.S. trade deficit, particularly with Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Two decades later the focus has swung to the record U.S. trade deficit, partly driven by Chinese exports and Beijing's policy of keeping the yuan's value tied closely to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In late Tokyo trade on Monday, the yen hovered around 119.40 per dollar <JPY=>, not far from its weakest since August 2003, and a long way from a five-year peak near 101.65 hit in January.  \par \par \uc2 The yen hit a new eight-year low against the New Zealand dollar near 82 <NZDJPY=R> and was close to lifetime lows against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the yen's weakness also reflects the sheer amount of carry trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where speculators and investors borrow yen at nearly no cost and sell it to buy other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their higher yields.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=119.16 Yen) (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-YEN|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|J|NAT|U|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051121e1bl000jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
