\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Ends Lower; Suspected Govt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2005\par 09:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won ended lower against the U.S. dollar Friday on short-covering triggered by suspected dollar buying by authorities.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at KRW1,002.5, up from KRW1,001.7 Thursday, after trading between KRW998.9 and KRW1,003.5.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S unit opened at KRW1,000.5 on offshore dollar selling and later extended its losses on export settlements.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar/won stayed above KRW999 on import settlements, but the pair recovered sharply on dollar short-covering in the last hour on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the central bankers of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China and Japan discussed ways to strengthen ties on the sidelines of an international conference on monetary policies.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea said the dollar/won trading volume was $3.80 billion, up from $2.71 billion Thursday and $2.10 billion Wednesday. The volume is usually around $4 billion on normal trading days.  \par \par \uc2 "But the market was thin throughout the week, and when you consider that, the BOK is suspected of having bought quite a lot (of dollars)," said a trader at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 BOK Governor Park Seung, Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui and People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan met for an hour after signing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap pact at the conference in Seoul.  \par \par \uc2 Despite high expectations for some discussions on a change in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, the three governors intentionally skipped \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related talk, "as the topic is sensitive now and we have the more urgent task of strengthening ties," Park told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Zhou declined to talk to reporters, but in his opening address to the conference he suggested Beijing's reforms will be gradual and careful in respect to the economic developments in the country as it transforms to a market economy from a centralized one. He didn't mention the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 His comments - a reiteration of what senior Chinese officials have been saying as the U.S. pressures it to revalue the yuan - will likely further damp speculation about the imminent strengthening in the Chinese and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect the dollar to trade between KRW998 and KRW1,005 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again showed that it can act swiftly to defend KRW1,000, so the local pair won't fall sharply below KRW1,000, but offshore players have been selling heavily around KRW1,002-3," said the foreign bank trader.  \par \par \uc2 In the options market, volatility implied by one-month at-the-money options edged down to 6.4%/6.8% from 6.5%/6.9% Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "There were more sellers in the shorter-term vols, but more bids in six months and longer as players want to hedge against possible risk in case there is the yuan revaluation," said an options trader at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 The one-month delta risk reversal, which indicates market bias, was in favor of dollar puts/won calls at 0.1%/0.5%, relatively unchanged from Thursday as the spot trade remained in a boxed range. [ 27-05-05 0749GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 South Korean government bond prices closed sharply lower after the finance minister expressed concern over a possible bubble forming in the real-estate sector.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on three-year treasurys rose eight basis points to close at 3.70%, while the five-year treasurys gained seven basis points to 3.84%. The 10-year treasury yield added six basis points to end at 4.33%.  \par \par \uc2 "Although inflation has been successfully managed, the Korean economy has seen a substantial increase in housing prices," Minister of Finance and Economy Han Duck-Soo told a lunch meeting with participants of an international conference hosted by the Bank of Korea.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm aware of the debate on whether monetary policy should respond to an asset bubble. I hope to gain an answer (to that issue)", Han said.  \par \par \uc2 A trader at a local brokerage firm said Han's comment "strongly suggests that there won't be further easing in monetary policy, while opening up a possibility for tightening."  \par \par \uc2 Han's comments also hurt the futures market. The June contract plunged 24 ticks to 111.94 as foreigners took profits as well.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants expect the three-year treasury yield to trade in a range of 3.67%-3.77% next week.  \par \par \uc2 "If the industrial output data come out within expectations, the bond market will remain relatively predictable," said the trader.  \par \par \uc2 The National Statistical Office is scheduled to issue April industrial production data at 2230 GMT Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 The average of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires was that industrial output fell 0.2% on month after seasonal adjustment, swinging from a 3.8% gain in March, because export growth weakened and private consumption remained depressed. On year, output likely rose 4.7% in April compared with a 4.8% gain in March.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                             Fri      Thu \par 3-Yr Treasury:             3.70% vs 3.62% \par 5-Yr Treasury:             3.84% vs 3.77% \par 10-Yr Treasury:            4.33% vs 4.27% \par  \par 1-Yr Stabilization Bond:   3.59% vs 3.55% \par 2-Yr Stabilization Bond:   3.70% vs 3.64% \par 3-Yr Corporate AA- Bonds:  4.11% vs 4.04% \par 3-Yr Corporate BBB- Bonds: 8.05% vs 7.98% \par  \par 3-Mo Koribor:              3.51% vs 3.51% \par  \par One-Day Call Rate:           -   vs 3.25% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Hye-Seung Seo and Jin-Young Yook, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; djnews.seoul@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 27-05-05 0830GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1063 1858  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050527e15r000ay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
