\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won ends flat, soft GDP data shrugged off  \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2005\par 08:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, May 20 (Reuters) - The South Korean won <KRW=> ended virtually flat on Friday, as much of the dollar sales by offshore players and exporters' deals were swallowed up by what dealers cited as government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market showed little reaction to data showing \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy grew at its weakest pace in nearly two years in the first quarter, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2  The won <KRW=KFTC> settled in local trade at 1,004.4 per dollar versus 1,005.0 at Thursday's close. It opened at 1,006.5.  \par \par \uc2  "Offshore selling, the dollar's weakness versus the yen and exporters' deals provided a lift to the won," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. "But authorities appeared firm in defending the dollar/won pair at around 1,004."  \par \par \uc2  Authorities, in a move to cap the won's strength, were estimated to have bought a minimum $1 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, dealers said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed early on Friday South Korea's economy grew 0.4 percent in the first quarter from the previous three months, as rising private spending failed to offset the impact of a slow down in exports.  \par \par \uc2  "Market players didn't pay much attention to the GDP data as they focused on the influx of dollars," said a dealer.  \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Bank of Korea said on Friday that it could consider using its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's fourth biggest, for loans to local banks over the longer term.  \par \par \uc2  Policy makers in South Korea has been looking at ways of managing the country's massive reserves, which stood at nearly $206 billion as of mid-May, more effectively.  \par \par \uc2  The yen <JPY=>, which the won closely follows, was quoted at 107.54/57 per dollar, versus 107.62 in late New York trade overnight and 107.11 in late Asia trade on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2  Seoul's benchmark stock index <.KS11> ended flat, with foreign investors buying a net 120.8 billion won worth of shares.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      0600 GMT        0045 GMT     prev close\par  Won <KRW=KFTC>          1,004.4         1,006.9        1,005.0\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>    9.3344/25       9.3418/72      9.3890/17\par  NDF 1-mth won       1,004.1/5.1     1,006.8/7.8    1,004.9/5.9\par  NDF 3-mth won       1,004.1/5.1     1,006.8/7.8    1,004.9/5.9\par  Stocks KOSPI <.KS11>     952.19          954.14         952.09\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050520e15k000xr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
