\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea bonds flat by mid-morning, won moves eyed.   \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 03:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, March 11 (Reuters) - South Korean government bond prices were flat by mid-morning on Friday, as investors sat tight to study the next direction of the market after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady and amid a volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Investors could sell bonds if the benchmark three-year yield fell close to 4.0 percent and amid caution after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 6 trillion won ($6 billion) worth of monetary stabilisation bonds via window sales in the morning, dealers said.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By 0142 GMT, the yield on three-year treasury bonds             was\par offered flat at 4.08 percent. The five-year treasury bond yield\par    was offered up one basis point at 4.31 percent, dealers said.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Sentiment was helped after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor said the bank will maintain a low interest rate policy, but bond investors are being pressured by the three-year yield level," a bond analyst said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Debt sentiment firmed up after Bank of Korea Governor Park Seung said on Thursday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would keep rate policy supportive of a smooth economic recovery. But he also expressed more optimism about the prospects for South Korea's economy, Asia's third largest.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fears about bond supply eased somewhat after a senior finance ministry official said on Thursday the government had enough funds for its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, allaying fears of a heavy treasuries issuance to fund \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won's movements are still being closely monitored by the debt market. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers said the government interevened to the tune of $2 billion on Thursday to curb the won's strength.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the supply front, the Bank of Korea held window sales to sell 2 trillion won in 140-day MSBs at a yield of 3.55 percent, 1.5 trillion won in 364-day paper at 3.85 percent and 2.5 trillion won in 546-day MSBs at 3.98 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the Korea Futures Exchange, the key March contract for three-year treasuries fell one tick to 110.76.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the local stock market, the benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index rose 1.94 percent. ($1=1000.2 Won).    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050311e13b000up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
