\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Down on China comments, Fed and G7.  \b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2005\par 04:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, Jan 31 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were weaker\par on Monday after fresh comments from Chinese top officials\par suggested they were in no hurry to reform the yuan regime, and\par the U.S. dollar found support ahead of key data and a Federal\par Reserve  policy meeting.\par     Traders braced for a volatile week that will end with the\par Group of Seven nations' meeting in London on Friday and\par Saturday and could see U.S. rates raised by another 25 basis\par points.\par     Global oil prices fell, with London's Brent crude futures\par down near $44.50 a barrel, after OPEC producers left output\par unchanged and Iraq's election passed without disruption to oil\par exports.\par     The Korean won Singapore dollar Thai baht and Indonesian\par rupiah were down a quarter percent each. The premium on\par one-year yuan in non-deliverable forwards was down to 3,850\par points, pricing in 4.9 percent gains, compared with 5.6 percent\par on Friday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "The dollar will bounce this week," said Claudio Piron,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with JP Morgan.\par     "Oil prices are lower and that should help the dollar. The\par Fed FOMC will be delivering 25 basis points and positioning in\par Asia is very aggressive," he said.\par     Markets also braced for the G7 to issue another standard\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 statement calling for general \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility, even though  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 officials from China, India and other emerging economies have  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 been invited to talks with the seven industrialised nations.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The see-sawing on the prospects of China revaluing its  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 virtually pegged yuan continued, with China's deputy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central  \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Li Ruogu dashing expectations by saying China did  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 not have the capacity to address global economic imbalances and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 was not willing or able to do it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 YEN-WON CROSS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Piron said the north Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would suffer, but  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 that markets would be divided on whether to buy or sell the won  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 against the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The won, Asia's best performer in 2004, opened near  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's 7-year peak of 1,023 per dollar and then fell. The  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 yen-won cross rate showed the yen at 9.91 won.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets have been torn between the urge to buy the yen-won  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 cross below the 10 mark - which they believe is the highest  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 level for the won Korean authorities can tolerate - and the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 belief that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily, since the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 government is running out of bonds to offset the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders waited for data on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korea for evidence of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in January and confirmation  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of their view that Korea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less in order to stabilise  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 its bond markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The finance ministry's announcement on Friday that it would  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 issue 5 trillion won in fiscal bonds in February, however,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 frustrated some won bulls. That came after Korea said it would  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 cut down on the amount of treasuries issued in February.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     To some, the finance ministry announcement was a covert\par warning to markets against pushing the won higher.\par     ""Certainly think they wanted to make that message and they\par made that message at the beginning of the year as well," Piron\par said.\par     "The difficulty is the market is still quite heavy against\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the dollar," he said, adding the Korean authorities were  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 fighting a market that was heavily biased in favour of the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 won.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0332 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       103.58         103.16       -0.41\par  Sing dlr        1.6394         1.6358       -0.22\par  Taiwan dlr      31.830         31.802       -0.09\par  Korean won     1027.10        1023.50       -0.35\par  Baht             38.55          38.45       -0.26\par  Peso             55.09          55.09       +0.01\par  Rupiah         9163.00        9144.00       -0.21\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       103.58         102.45       -1.09\par  Sing dlr        1.6394    