\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won hits 7-yr high on yuan, export deals.  \b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2005\par 09:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     SEOUL, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The South Korean won darted to\par seven-year highs on Friday, lifted by exporters' dollar sales and\par amid renewed speculation over whether China would move toward a\par more flexible exchange rate regime.\par     The won                   settled domestic trade at 1,024.1\par per dollar, versus 1,028.7 at Thursday's close. It touched\par 1,024.0 just before the close, its highest since hitting 1,012.0\par on Nov. 18, 1997.\par     "The dollar will remain under pressure with the Lunar New\par Year holidays coming up, prior to which, exporters will continue\par to close their books by selling the U.S. unit," local dealers\par said.\par     Comments about China's yuan have sparked waves of buying and\par selling in recent weeks, and analysts say the trend will continue\par ahead of the Group of Seven rich nations meet in London Feb. 4-5,\par where China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime will be a hot topic.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     A Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said on Friday China would\par keep its yuan stable but take gradual steps to perfect the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime.\par     That sapped renewed hopes of a revaluation after Yu Yongding,\par a leading Chinese economist and member of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par monetary policy committee, said the time was ripe for Beijing to\par move gradually toward a more flexible exchange rate regime.\par     If China relaxes its yuan peg -- currently at 8.28 to the\par dollar -- a broad-based rise in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of China's Asian\par trading partners is expected to follow suit.\par     Traders added many players settled their long-positions on\par the dollar ahead of the weekend late in the session, while many\par remained cautious over government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is known to favour a cheaper won to maintain the\par price competitiveness of local exports, the sole driver of\par economic growth during a two-year slump in domestic demand.\par     The won also found support from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data showing\par South Korea's current account surplus in December widened to $2\par billion from a $1.89 billion surplus a year earlier.\par     But sentiment cooled slightly on separate data showing the\par country's factory output fell worse than expected 1.9 percent in\par December, denting hopes exports would stay sturdy until a broad\par recovery in domestic consumption had begun.\par     The yen       , which the won closely follows, quoted at\par 103.17, after seesawing since 103.04 in late New York trade\par Thursday.\par     The market was also awaiting U.S. fourth-quarter gross\par domestic product figures with analysts expecting a rise of 3.5\par percent. Traders are also on the lookout for U.S. jobs data and\par possible rates change by Federal Reserve Board next week.\par  \par     The benchmark stock index         down 0.4 percent, with\par foreign investors selling a net 75.7 billion won worth.\par                           0730 GMT       0020 GMT    prev close\par  Won                       1,024.1        1,026.3       1,028.7\par  *Yen/won                9.9301/08      9.9679/57     9.9535/13\par  ~NDF 1-mth won        1,024.0/5.0    1,026.4/7.4   1,028.9/9.9\par  ~NDF 3-mth won        1,024.7/5.7    1,027.1/8.1  1,029.7/30.7\par  Stoc