\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Korea Keen To Establish Euro Bond Benchmark  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Lane  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 825 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2005\par 09:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The South Korean government is considering issuing in euros for its next international bond to help establish a pricing benchmark for the growing number of local firms keen to issue in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Speaking to Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of a recent Citigroup investor conference in Hong Kong, Yeo Kwon Yoon, director of the international finance bureau within South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy, said, "These days, there are many Korean companies or banks that want to go to the euro market."  \par \par \uc2 "There is some demand to make a benchmark in the euro market as well as the dollar market."  \par \par \uc2 The government said late last year that it was considering denominating its 2005 global bond in the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , following a high-profile EUR1 billion bond sale by the Chinese government. China attracted hefty demand for the bond and priced it through comparable U.S. dollar-denominated paper.  \par \par \uc2 Size-wise, issuance this year will be the same as in 2004 and in 2003 with the National Assembly having given approval for the Korean government to sell $1 billion equivalent in international bonds this year.  \par \par \uc2 No decision has been made on the structure and terms of the proposed bond, but Yoon said that "our mandate is just the amount. We have a free hand in which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to issue."  \par \par \uc2 Yoon said that a number of investment banks have been pitching proposals to issue in euros. The ministry will submit its issuance plan for this year to the president in mid February, slightly later than expected.  \par \par \uc2 Market conditions currently favor issuance in euros, but Yoon said that could change very quickly.  \par \par \uc2 Among Korean banks and companies seeking to issue in euros is state-run Korean Development Bank which is preparing a EUR500 million issue via ABN Amro and Deutsche Bank. KDB is set to sell the bond next week, having set its preferred price earlier Friday while meeting international investors.  \par \par \uc2 Euro-denominated bonds were also among the ideas submitted last week to the Korea Exchange Bank which is expected to come to the market later this quarter.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Domestic Issuance Still On Track \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also on the cards is a long-dated $300 million deal from Shinhan Bank, suggesting that a Korea sovereign sale may not be in the offing until closer to mid year at the earliest.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Korean government last tapped the international bond market last September with a $1 billion bond that attracted over $3 billion in orders. Priced at 85 basis points over U.S. Treasurys, it is currently trading around 72 basis points over U.S. government debt.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yoon said the amount the government raises through the international bond markets would not be affected by its fund-raising domestically since each is fixed separately.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government recently cut issuance of treasurys for next month to barely a third of January's as yields, particularly at the middle to longer end, soared after the Bank of Korea kept its policy interest rate unchanged two weeks ago. There were expectations the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would cut rates to stimulate the fragile economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yoon said that despite the cut in supply next month, the government will still likely sell a planned KRW59.4 trillion in domestic bonds this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ministry and the Bank of Korea agree the recent rise in yields was "groundless," and stemmed mainly from January's hefty supply, he said. What concerned the two was the speed of the increase. "In one to two weeks, the bond yield went up 50 basis points. That is too fast," he said. "If the fluctuation in yields isn't sharp, there is not much concern for the ministry."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As such, he forecast the government would continue to sell around KRW5 trillion in bonds each month in a continued effort to expand fiscal policy to boost domestic demand.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yoon noted that while credit card volumes and department store sales were improving they were "still very weak."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea is an alternative buyer of government bonds should the government not want to sell to the market proper.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last week, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would buy KRW1 trillion of treasurys to stabilize the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News of the cut in February bond sales sent the won higher against the U.S. dollar as the market reckons the government's ability to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to suppress won strength may have diminished.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yoon shrugged off any long-term won appreciation, however, saying a one-month decrease in bond issuance wouldn't have any sustained impact.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , like many Asian countries, has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to suppress the value of the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to keep exports competitive.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Karen Lane, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4043; karen.lane@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Lim Mui Khi \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 28-01-05 0856GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050128e11s000cw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
