\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Ends Lower On Yen Slide, Govt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 08:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won finished lower Friday following the dollar's recovery from a five-year low against the yen Thursday. Authorities' purchases also helped the greenback sustain its gains despite continued selling by exporters, market participants said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at 1,045.5 won, up from its close of KRW1,041.5 Thursday, the lowest since Nov. 19, 1997. It traded from KRW1,044.1 to KRW1,048.0 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit remained in positive territory all day, starting off as high as KRW1,048 after it regained above the Y103 level overnight on short covering on speculation that U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due later Friday might be stronger than expected.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar's fall against the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't as dramatic as the yen, with players hesitant to build dollar-long positions against the won for fear of spurring more speculative selling by exporters.  \par \par \uc2 At 0740 GMT, the dollar was at Y103.97, up sharply from a low of Y101.86 in Japan a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 "It wasn't that there were many buyers in the market, but more of less selling by exporters ahead of the U.S. payrolls data," said a dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 Buying of dollars by South Korean authorities persisted throughout the day to absorb the dollar supplies from exporters and sustain the U.S. unit's rising momentum.  \par \par \uc2 "Authorities must have sighed in relief. If not for the (decline of the) yen, we could have seen the break of the 1,040 level today," a local bank dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The won has repeatedly hit seven-year highs this week. The local unit has risen despite repeated warnings from government officials, including President Roh Moo-Hyun, that they will act - possibly jointly with Japan - to slow the won's gains to lessen the negative impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-reliant economy.  \par \par \uc2 "These warnings and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments failed to stir the market because authorities' actions won't be able to match the strong words due to limited (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) funds left," another local dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, the government said it will issue an additional KRW1 trillion in three-year treasurys Monday to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Including the new issue, the government would have issued a total of KRW18 trillion in treasurys earmarked for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, leaving just KRW800 billion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  budget for the full year.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. nonfarm payrolls data for November are expected to show the economy added 218,000 new jobs, according to a Dow Jones Newswires survey of economists. The U.S. economy created 337,000 new jobs in the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 If the data are better than expected, the dollar could rally to 105.0 against the yen, and above 1,050 versus the won, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Investors largely kept to the sidelines in the options market on the belief that the 1,040-1,050 range would prevail in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 The volatility implied by one-month at-the-money dollar/won options was 8.9%/9.6%, hardly changed from the previous day.  \par \par \uc2 "The options market is usually quiet in December. Without a spot price break of 1,030, this year won't be an exception," said an options dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 The one-month delta risk reversal - which indicates market bias - still favored dollar puts/won calls at 1.4%/1.9%, a tad lower than 1.5%/2.0% a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar to trade between KRW1,040.0 and KRW1,050.0 in the spot market Monday. [ 03-12-04 0757GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 South Korean treasurys ended mostly flat Friday due to continued pressure from the tight spread between the overnight call rate target and the three-year government bond yield, said market participants.  \par \par \uc2 The three- and five-year treasury yields finished unchanged at 3.29% and 3.43%, respectively.  \par \par \uc2 "The narrow spread between the policy rate and the three-year treasury yield pressured the market, while players were losing confidence over (the likelihood of) an additional rate cut" when the monetary policy committee meets Thursday, said Kim Nam-Soo, a fund manager at Kyobo Investment Trust.  \par \par \uc2 The news of the extra KRW1 trillion three-year treasury issue by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy Monday to raise funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little impact on bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Including this additional issue, the finance ministry is slated to issue a total of KRW2.08 trillion in treasurys Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The market had already expected the news, so no one was surprised by the announcement," said Yang Jin-Mo, an analyst at SK Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "Rather, the government's plan to offer more bonds for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market action eased uncertainty over excessive supply. So the market will keep to the sidelines until the BOK decides on the policy rate Thursday," said Kim at Kyobo.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the widely traded three-year treasury yield to range between 3.28% and 3.31% Monday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                Fri      Thu \par 3-Yr Treasury:                 3.29% vs 3.29% \par 5-Yr Treasury:                 3.43% vs 3.43% \par 10-Yr Treasury:                3.93% vs 3.92% \par  \par 1-Yr Stabilization Bond:       3.28% vs 3.28% \par 2-Yr Stabilization Bond:       3.33% vs 3.34% \par 3-Yr Corporate AA- Bonds:      3.75% vs 3.75% \par 3-Yr Corporate BBB- Bonds:     8.16% vs 8.16% \par  \par 3-Mo Koribor:                  3.36% vs 3.36% \par  \par One-Day Call Rate:               -   vs 3.24% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Hye-Seung Seo and Soo-kyung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; djnews.seoul@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lena Lee [ 03-12-04 0859GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1063 1858  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041203e0c30009k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
