\par\par \b \uc2 Bk Of Korea Bd Member: \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Has Eased Inflation Pressure  \b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2004\par 13:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By In Kyung Kim Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--A member of the Bank of Korea's monetary policy committee Wednesday said inflationary pressures have eased while economic growth is expected to decelerate next year, comments likely to boost mounting expectations for an interest-rate cut.  \par \par \uc2 Kim Tae-Dong also told Dow Jones Newswires that the Bank's surprise rate cuts in August and November have boosted the economy, countering arguments by private economists that the moves have been ineffective.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Wednesday, South Korean treasury prices surged to new highs on renewed expectations for further interest-rate cuts, after the consumer price index showed November inflation was softer than expected.  \par \par \uc2 "At the time of the November rate decision, oil prices had declined while the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate (of the dollar to the Korean won) had plunged, helping inflation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate has gone even further down since then, while oil prices have maintained similar levels," said Kim, whose four-year term as a committee member ends in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, economic growth is expected to be "close" to the 5% potential growth rate this year, but fall short of that rate by 1 percentage point next year, he said, echoing remarks Tuesday by Minister of Finance and Economy Lee Hun-Jai.  \par \par \uc2 The government has pinned its hopes on attaining 5% growth this year - the level at which Korea's economy is considered able to expand in the long run without triggering excessive inflation. But many investment banks forecast that the South Korean economy will grow at the 4% level this year before expanding at the 3% level next year, triggering possible job losses and a prolonged slowdown.  \par \par \uc2 Kim said his foremost concern is stabilizing inflation, while economic growth is secondary. He said he is confident that core inflation won't break the 2.5%-3.5% target band set for 2004-2006. This year, core inflation is likely to reach 3.0%, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Kim said the Bank of Korea's interest-rate cuts in August and November have been effective, countering market sentiment that their impact on the economy has been muted. The Bank of Korea defied market expectations, and bucked a global tightening trend, when it twice eased rates by 25 basis points, to a record low 3.25%.  \par \par \uc2 Kim said monetary easing rarely has any impact on capital investment in any country, so lack of impact there isn't unique to Korea. As for reviving private spending, he said large household debt has weakened the impact on consumption, but lower rates will nudge consumers to spend once \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's debt overhang eases.  \par \par \uc2 Kim, however, also blamed government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for reducing the impact of the rate cuts.  \par \par \uc2 In an open economy, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should decline after a monetary easing, boosting exports, but market forces weren't fully functioning because of the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the dollar fell, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate would have fallen even more if there had been no monetary easing," he added.  \par \par \uc2 On criticism that the Bank of Korea doesn't give sufficient signals to the market before its rate decisions, Kim said the market isn't diligent enough in watching for the BOK's signals.  \par \par \uc2 "Just look at the BOK's rate cut pattern; look at when the second rate cut occurred after the first cut. If there was no rate cut two months after the first cut, what's the probability that there would have been a rate cut in the following month?" he said. Last year, the Bank of Korea cut interest rates by quarter points each in May and July.  \par \par \uc2 In October, BOK Governor Park Seung berated the bond market for betting heavily in favor of a rate cut, calling it "overheated" and in need of "more training." These comments led the market to wrongly assume that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would shelve its easing bias until 2005.  \par \par \uc2 As for the government, which has openly called for more interest-rate cuts to revive the economy, Kim said the government needs to be "more aggressive" with fiscal policy. However, he recommended tax cuts rather than increased spending.  \par \par \uc2 Under current circumstances, when national transparency is quite low, there's a possibility that government money won't flow into high-productivity investments, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked whether he generally favors higher or lower interest rates, Kim said, "I can't say whether I'm hawkish or dovish, that's for you to decide, but I argued for a rate cut between February and April 2003, six to one before monetary easing in May," he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By In Kyung Kim, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; in-kyung.kim@dowjones.com [ 01-12-04 1228GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041201e0c1000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
