\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Asia must share  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  pain with euro - OECD's Cotis.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Love  \par \uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2004\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 PARIS, Nov 30 (Reuters) - The euro zone has paid its share of the economic bill for the slide in the dollar and Asia must now do so by letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise versus the greenback, OECD chief economist Jean-Philippe Cotis says.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters, Cotis said that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should avoid raising interest rates, probably until 2006, to help the 12-nation euro area economy weather a surge in world oil prices and the impact of a sharp drop in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Cotis's recommendations to Asia come at a time of renewed market fears that Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  or other Asian countries may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too much on the grounds that their exports and growth are threatened.  \par \par \uc2 "It's an anomoly to ask the weakspot of the world economy to do all the (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) appreciation," Cotis, who presented the Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development's report on the economy of the 30-country OECD on Tuesday, said.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem to be appreciating also alongside the euro is for us a good beginning, a sign of a better readjustment of current account imbalances," he said, making it clear he hoped Asia would steer clear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this time round and let \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has surged to records against the dollar in recent days as markets sign up to the idea that Washington is not unhappy with a dollar slide that could help U.S. exports and trim a record U.S. trade deficit regarded as unsustainable.  \par \par \uc2 Cotis said further rises could be very damaging for the euro zone but he hoped he had detected signs of greater willingness in Asia to let \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  there rise too, sharing the burden this imposes by making their exports relatively more costly.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Governments in countries such as Japan and South Korea have watched the\par yen  and won       soar to four-and-a-half and seven-year highs\par respectively in recent days but refrained from past practice of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the markets to drive their money back down in value and\par thereby protect their exports.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cotis declined to venture an estimate of what level might be excessive for the euro's rise against the dollar - it soared above $1.33 last week - preferring to stress the need for an orderly adjustment where all regions helped absorb the change.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB RATES ON HOLD THROUGH 2005?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not so long ago markets were convinced that an interest rate rise from the ECB was as good as inevitable in 2005, but that was before the oil price spike, dollar slide and news of poor growth in the third quarter in the euro zone. "Should the appreciation of the euro get out of proportion - and knowing that the recovery so far has been export driven and there's little domestic momentum in the recovery - I guess there'd be a case for relaxation (of ECB rates)," Cotis said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But I don't think we are there yet. There is a strong case for not tightening and the option of relaxing is in case of a catastrophic outcome on the exchange rate side."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He highlighted that the recent surge in the euro and Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  followed another bout of dollar weakness in early 2004, a time when the euro zone bore much of the brunt as Asian countries resisted with dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Now we seem to be moving towards a pattern of better shared adjustment worldwide with the Koren won appreciating, the yen appreciating, (although) the Chinese renmimbi is still missing from the list," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What we need is a worldwide effort to cure a worldwide problem. Basically allowing an appreciation is in the best long-term interests of the Asian economies."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The twice-yearly OECD report that he presented cut growth forecasts for the eurozone to 1.8 percent for 2004 from 2.0 percent and to 1.9 percent in 2005 from 2.4 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The OECD tweaked the U.S. growth forecast marginally upwards to 4.4 percent for this year and lowered it to 3.3 percent from 3.7 percent for next year, and cut Japan's growth outlook to 4.0 percent from 4.4 this year and to 2.1 from 2.8 for 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LIVING WITH DEARER OIL  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cotis put most of the changes down to the same thing, a surge in world oil prices to more than $50 a barrel in the third quarter of 2004, leaving them around 50 percent higher than at the start of the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Should oil prices stabilise, growth is going to accelerate. If it stops there, at current prices, I don't see that this would stand in the way of a continued recovery."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the late 1990s the world's oil consuming countries were keen to see the price of a barrel of oil remain around $25, but they could now live with a price of $40 or more, he said, as long as there was stability and no surges of the recent kind. The OECD includes all the world's rich industrialised economies as well as emerging market economies such as Turkey, Mexico and eastern European countries which have just joined the European Union.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041130e0bu006sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
