\par\par \b \uc2 DJ AWSJ(11/23) Bank Of S Korea Steps In To Slow Surge Of Won  \b0\par\par\uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 Nov 22, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 (From THE ASIAN WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By Donald Greenlees SEOUL -- \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the appreciation of the won against the dollar, as concern mounted over the impact a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have on exports, the main driver of the country's economy. Yesterday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the second in less than two weeks by the Bank of Korea after it aggressively bought dollars on Nov. 10, traders said. In spite of that previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the won continued to strengthen, reaching a seven-year high against the dollar last week. A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a Seoul bank estimated that authorities yesterday purchased roughly $500 million to $600 million of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster its value following a meeting between Finance Minister Lee Hun Jai and Park Seung, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, to discuss measures to curb the won's rapid strengthening. Government officials are concerned the strong won will hurt exports of memory chips, consumer electronics, household appliances and automobiles, which are the mainstays of South Korea's economic growth, amid weak domestic consumption. A strong won will increase the dollar prices of exports, potentially reducing demand for them in the U.S. and elsewhere. Those same fears about a strong won pushed down shares of export-oriented manufacturing companies on the Korea Stock Exchange yesterday. The main index fell 17.04 points, or 2%, to close at 849.99. Markets elsewhere in Asia dropped on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns. In Japan, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average slid 2.1%, while in Taiwan, the benchmark index was down 3.1%. After opening at 1,068.70 won per dollar, the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to end the day at 1,065.30 per dollar, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the afternoon. Dealers said the relatively small size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggested that the bank was trying to slow the rate of the won's ascent rather than prevent further strengthening. The head of the Bank of Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team, Oh Jae Kwon, said he couldn't comment on "whether the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not." In his meeting with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Mr. Lee urged the bank to be "bolder and more aggressive" in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, according to a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy quoted by Dow Jones Newswires. The official said Mr. Lee suggested the bank print won to buy up dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Despite the two \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, economic analysts predict the Bank of Korea will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more firmly only if central banks in Japan and Taiwan also choose to come into the market to buy dollars. "My sense is that they don't have the appetite to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless they can coordinate it with other central banks in the region," said Michael Spencer, chief economist for Asia at Deutsche Bank in Hong Kong. The strength of the won, with the dangers that poses to Korean exports and economic growth, appears to be replacing inflation as the Korean government's major concern. Mr. Spencer predicted that the won's performance would prompt the Bank of Korea to make another quarter-percentage-point cut in interest rates by early 2005. This could help take some of the pressure off exporters, which might start to see profit margins squeezed in their results for the current quarter or the first three months of 2005. --- Seah Park contributed to this article.  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041122e0bm006pv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
